A||N||12||whamel||Removing paint from rims||||1||
Z||000000||whamel||08-21-2000||11:12 AM||||The rims on my GT were painted yellow when I bought it, and although I appreciate the effect this gives I have to admit I would like to change it.  What is the best way to get the paint off the rims without doing a tremendous amount of damage.  I really don't want to ruin them as they seem to be in good shape.  The tires are shot, so that's no big deal - I'm going to have to change those out anyway.  The way I see it, there's a few choices and I am not sure how to proceed at all.<BR>I though a chemical stripper might be a little too harsh for the wheels and take off the finish.<BR>I also thought the same thing about sanding them down by hand.  I don't mind the effort, but would this be worse in the long run?  I also considered using a light abrasive on them, but couldn't think of what might be 'light' enough.<BR>I can take them to a shop and have them stripped.  I have a spare set of rims and tires for the car, so that wouldn't be a real big deal.  I think this might be the best option.  Any thoughts?  I don't really want to turn to a shop if I don't have to - that gets expen$ive pretty quick sometimes.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||coyote||08-21-2000||11:35 AM||||My weopon of choice was Permatex gasket remover, it will require a little elbow grease to get the paint out of the nooks and cranies. But it works really well.<P>Coyote||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||08-21-2000||11:44 AM||||Here's a page I found on refurbishing GT rims.  Don't know if it's what your looking for, but it might give you ideas...<P><A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/wheel.htm" TARGET=_blank>Clean and Polish your wheels</A><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000003||whamel||08-21-2000||12:43 PM||||I think I'll take a look at that gasket remover first and try that out, but the stuff on the web page was very good advice too.  I guess I can try it on one rim and see what happens first.  If it doesn't look too bad after a day or so, then I can go from there!<BR>Thanks!<BR>Wade<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ourufo||08-23-2000||07:26 AM||||Has anyone tried HOTTANKING? it's done to heads.<P>------------------<BR>OURUFOV8||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||08-23-2000||12:26 PM||||I seem to remember you can't tank aluminum. The stuff is corosive to it.<P>Any chemical paint remover will work. Just follow the directions on it. The citrus strippers are propular these days. A little slower but far less toxic to you and the environment. If you are leaving the tires mounted the citrus won't hurt them like some of the chemical ones can.<P>If you want the best job you need to remove the tires from the rims.<P>You have a few options to refinish them. (In order of cost.) Paint, Powder coating, or Anodize.<P>Wheel paint gives you the same result as they came. You use regular auto paint for color then shoot clear wheel paint over it.<P>Powder can be used a number of ways. You can even produce a factory looking paint style but it will be nealy indestructable. Powder is far more resistant than paint to chemicals, acid rain, etc. Unless you hit a curb.... Then you'll scrap any finish up, even anodize. You can get powder coating equipment from Eastwood for about $250, or send it out.<P>Anodize produces even tougher finish and can give some amazing results depending on how it's applied and what's done after application. It's expencive as hell tho. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||KitKar||08-23-2000||12:54 PM||||Is there any reason why you can't sand blast them?<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||08-23-2000||04:29 PM||||Anodizing looks cooooooooool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'd be careful with sandblasting, it might be a little too abrasive to use on the wheels.  I think somebody on the forum bead-blasted their wheels, tho.  I'll look for the thread.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||whamel||08-23-2000||04:33 PM||||I think I'm just going to go with the 'ole fashion way and use a light chemical and a wire brush.  I think that would probably be the safest way to clean them.  If I screw one up, I've already got a spare set ready to go.  <BR>I do appreciate all the advice though!  As always, very informative.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||08-24-2000||02:34 AM||||Don't use a wire brush it will damage the aluminum. Use a brass bristled brush. Brass brushes won't mar the surface of the aluminum.||||reg||4
Z||000010||theogre||08-24-2000||11:36 AM||||Sand blasting is probly to abrasive for aluminum. Fine glass bead blasting might work. Same process basically as sand but less abrasive and easier to control.<P>Been my experience with stripper you shouldn't need any kind of wire brush but if you do definatly use brass on AL. Mostly strippers are put it on, let it sit until it eats thru the finish, hose off. Just follow the directions on the can.<P>There's a 4th finish option that I did once.... strip the wheel, paint the colored ares and leave the AL uncoated. I just cleaned them with Mothers every couple months. Mothers AL cleaner/polish leaves a slight film that makes the brake dust have a harder time sticking. We don't get huge amounts of road salt here tho. This might not be a good option in areas with hard winter or near the shore.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||whamel||08-24-2000||11:40 AM||||whoops - maybe I should have been more specific. I have a mag wheel brush that I never use.  It's soft, not wire.  I figure if the stripper eats it, who cares.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fropuff||08-24-2000||06:52 PM||||I tried the stripping that Ogre was talking about.  I used some plastic chemical stripper on the clearcoat on the aluminum, and it turned out great!  I will do the other wheels tomorrow.  Then, I put some Mother's polish over the aluminum, and it looks as good as new.  I might clearcoat later, but I'll keep it for now.||||reg||10
